WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.140
Serialization is not only input.

2
00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.212
Output serialization can leak data too.

3
00:00:04.292 --> 00:00:07.924
A Java entity may contain password hashes,
reset tokens, internal roles,

4
00:00:08.004 --> 00:00:11.284
tenant IDs, audit metadata, private notes,
service credentials,

5
00:00:11.364 --> 00:00:12.711
or configuration details.

6
00:00:12.791 --> 00:00:15.720
If the application serializes the entity
directly to JSON,

7
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.612
a response can expose fields the caller
should never see.

8
00:00:18.692 --> 00:00:22.148
Safe output mapping is a first-class part
of serialization security.

9
00:00:22.228 --> 00:00:24.220
Use response DTOs, views, serializers,

10
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.287
or explicit mapping to control what leaves
the application.

11
00:00:27.367 --> 00:00:30.530
Avoid serializing secrets into messages,
logs, cache entries,

12
00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:33.891
client responses, debug dumps, support
bundles, exported files,

13
00:00:33.971 --> 00:00:37.544
or dead-letter stores unless there is a
clear need and a protection plan.

14
00:00:37.624 --> 00:00:39.909
Encryption may protect storage or
transport,

15
00:00:39.989 --> 00:00:44.265
but it does not replace minimization,
authorization, redaction, and access control.

16
00:00:44.345 --> 00:00:46.454
Logs are a common secondary exposure path.

17
00:00:46.534 --> 00:00:50.341
Redact sensitive fields before logging
parse errors, validation failures,

18
00:00:50.421 --> 00:00:53.584
rejected messages, raw payload samples, or
exception details.

19
00:00:53.664 --> 00:00:56.945
Do not log full serialized payloads when
they may contain secrets,

20
00:00:57.025 --> 00:01:00.539
tokens, authorization headers, cookies,
private URLs, credentials,

21
00:01:00.619 --> 00:01:03.138
sensitive records, or unnecessary personal
data.

22
00:01:03.218 --> 00:01:07.377
A safe log should help operators
investigate without preserving the sensitive data

23
00:01:07.457 --> 00:01:08.688
that caused the concern.

24
00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:12.692
Dead-letter queues, failed-message stores,
caches, traces, object storage,

25
00:01:12.772 --> 00:01:15.525
and support bundles can become
sensitive-data stores.

26
00:01:15.605 --> 00:01:19.940
Retention and deletion expectations should
be defined for serialized sensitive data.

27
00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:23.886
Review who can access those stores, how
long data remains, what gets replayed,

28
00:01:23.966 --> 00:01:26.661
and what must be rotated or remediated
after exposure.

29
00:01:26.741 --> 00:01:30.900
Serialization can copy data widely;
minimization keeps that spread under control.
